<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-poisson-DRV" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Poisson Random Variable</title>

<introduction>
 <p> 
    By completing the problems provided below, you will gain experience using the Poisson random variable. Specifically, you will practice: 
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Identifying when the Poisson distribution can be used to model a scenario.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Using the Poisson probability distribution function to compute probabilities.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
            Determining the Poisson rate parameter for a specified interval.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
            Using the expected value and variance formulas for a Poisson random variable.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</introduction>


 <exercises xml:id="hw-poisson-rv" >
   <title>Interactive Exercises</title>


<exercise label="ex-is-it-poisson">
  <title>Is it Poisson?</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Which of the following random variables <m>Z</m> can be appropriately modeled using a 
      Poisson distribution? Select all that apply.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <!-- CORRECT: Classic temporal Poisson process -->
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A university IT help desk receives support tickets  at an  
          average rate of <m>4.5</m> tickets per hour during weekday mornings. Let <m>Z</m> 
          be the number of tickets received between 8:30AM and 11:30AM
          on a randomly chosen Tuesday.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct! Tickets arrive at a constant average rate over a fixed, 
          continuous time interval, and <m>Z </m> counts the number of occurrences.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- CORRECT: Spatial Poisson process -->
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Minor typographical errors occur randomly throughout a <m>500</m>-page 
          manuscript at an average rate of <m>3</m> typos per 5 pages. Let <m>Z</m> be the total 
          number of typos found in a randomly selected <m>5</m>-page section.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct! A Poisson process can occur across space, just as it does 
          across time. Here, typos occur at a constant average rate over a fixed <m>5</m>-page interval, and <m>Z </m> counts the number of occurrences.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 1: Clustered / simultaneous arrivals (violates independence & single-occurrence) -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Customers arrive at a family restaurant for dinner, mostly in groups of <m>2</m> to 
          <m>6</m> people together. On average, <m>18</m> customers enter the restaurant 
          per hour. Let <m>Z
          </m> be the number of individual customers who enter between 
          <m>6:00\text{ PM}</m> and <m>7:00\text{ PM}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. Because customers arrive in groups rather than individually, counts are clustered, and the independence assumption is violated. 
           While the number of dining parties might 
          be Poisson, the number of <em>individual</em>  customers is not.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 2: Non-homogeneous / time-varying rate -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Cars pass through a highway toll plaza at an average rate of <m>20</m> cars per hour 
          during late-night hours, surging to <m>150</m> cars per hour during morning rush hour. 
          Let <m>Z</m> be the number of cars passing through during a randomly selected 
          <m>1</m>-hour window of the day.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. The arrival rate is not constant. It changes dramatically 
          between late night and rush hour. A standard Poisson model requires a constant 
          average rate <m>\lambda</m> over the entire observation window.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 3: Exponential waiting time (continuous vs. discrete count) -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Radioactive particles emitted by a sample are detected by a Geiger counter independently 
          at a constant average rate of <m>3.2</m> particles per minute. Let <m>Z</m> be the 
          elapsed time (in minutes) until the next particle is detected.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. Even though the underlying arrivals form a Poisson process, <m>Z</m> measures 
          a continuous <em>waiting time</em>, rather than a discrete <em>count</em> of events. 
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <!-- DISTRACTOR 4: Bounded Binomial with small n and high p -->
    <choice correct="no">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A commuter passes through <m>4</m> independent traffic lights on the way to work, 
          each of which is red with probability <m>0.50</m>. Let <m>Z</m> be the number of 
          red lights the commuter hits on a morning drive.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. <m>Z</m> is Binomial with <m>n = 4</m> 
          (traffic lights) and <m>p = 0.50</m>. We are considering a fixed number of trials here, rather than a continuous interval of time or space. 
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>

  <hint>
    
    <p>
      To verify if a random variable <m>Z</m> follows a Poisson distribution, check for these  
      conditions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Discrete count in a continuous interval: <m>Z</m> counts the number of 
          occurrences of an event within a fixed, continuous, interval of time or space.
        </p>
      </li>
      
      <li>
        <p>
          Constant rate of Occurrence: The average rate of occurrence (<m>\lambda</m>)
          is proportional to the length/size of the interval and does not otherwise change over time or space.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Independence: Events occur independently of one another, i.e., the number of 
          occurrences in one interval does not affect the number in any non-overlapping interval.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </hint>
</exercise>






<exercise label="ex-poisson-er-triage">
    <title>Emergency Room Patient Intake</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            During a busy weekend shift, trauma patients arrive at an urban emergency room according to a Poisson process with a mean rate of 45 patients per hour. If the front desk nurse steps away to grab a clean stethoscope, leaving the desk unattended for two minutes, what is the probability that more than 1 patient arrives during that brief interval?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.7769</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the probability that 1 or more patients arrive during the two-minute window (<m>P(Z \ge 1)</m>). 
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
         <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.5578</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the probability that at most 1 patient arrives during the two-minute window (<m>P(Z \le 1)</m>). 
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p><m>0.4422</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! 
                    First, convert the hourly arrival rate to a 2-minute arrival rate: <me>\lambda = \frac{45 \text{ patients}}{60 \text{ minutes}}\times 2 =  1.5</me>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Then, apply the complement rule: 
                    <me>P(Z \gt 1) = 1 - [P(Z = 0)+P(Z=1)] = 1 - \left[\frac{e^{-1.5}(1.5)^0 }{0!} + \frac{e^{-1.5}(1.5)^1 }{1!}\right]= 1 - 0.5578 = 0.4422</me>
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.3347</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the probability that one patient arrives (<m>P(Z=1)</m>) during the two-minute time window. 
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement><p><m>0.1734</m></p></statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. It's possible that you did not properly scale your rate <m>\lambda</m>. Double-check your calculation for converting 45 patients per hour down to a two-minute time interval.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>
</exercise>



<exercise label="ex-poisson-campus-security">
    <title>Campus Security Patrols</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Suppose that a campus security department devises a randomized patrol pattern to ensure unpredictability. A security vehicle may visit a high-priority quad location <m>Y = 0, 1, 2, 3, \dots</m> times per hour  during the night shift.
            Over a long observation window, records show each location is visited an average of once per hour. Assume that <m>Y</m> follows a Poisson probability distribution.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Task 1: Identification Criteria & Lambda Identification -->
 <!--   <task xml:id="task-poisson-id">
        <statement>
            <p>
                How could you identify that <m>Y</m> is a Poisson random variable if that information had not been  provided? 
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable counts random, independent events occurring over a fixed continuum of time, where the rate is proportional to the interval size. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! The Poisson distribution models the counts of independent occurrences over a continuous interval (like an hour) given a known average rate (<m>\lambda = 1</m> visit per hour).</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable counts independent trials that can only result in binary success or failure outcomes from a fixed set of trials. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. That description fits a Binomial distribution. Poisson distributions do not have a fixed upper limit of discrete trials.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p>The variable tracks the total number of consecutive trials required until the very first success is observed. </p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. That description defines a Geometric random variable. Here, we are counting occurrences within a standard time block.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>The visits have a uniform chance of happening at any point, meaning the count is steady. </p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. Uniform distributions track spatial or temporal position variations, not accumulated integer incidence tallies. Also, \(\lambda\) is the mean rate, which is 1.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
<choice>
                <statement><p>It tracks changing success probabilities across consecutive non-replacement choices. </p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Incorrect. That description applies to a Hypergeometric distribution, not a time-series event sequence.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task> -->

    <!-- Task 2: Calculate Missing Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-miss">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the probability that the campus security officer will completely miss a given high-priority location during a specific one-hour time period.  Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
        <p>
       <m>P(Z = 0) =</m> <fillin width="8" answer="0.368"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the Poisson probability distribution function <m>P(Z = z) = \frac{ e^{-\lambda} \lambda^z}{z!}</m> with parameter <m>\lambda = 1</m>:
                <me>P(Z = 0) = \frac{  e^{-1}1^0}{0!} = e^{-1} \approx 0.368</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Task 3: Calculate More Than Once Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-poisson-more">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 What is the probability that the location will be visited twice during the one-hour time period? Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
            </p>
               <p>
        <m>P(Z = 2) =</m><fillin width="8" answer="0.184"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct!          
                <me>P(Z=2) = \frac{  e^{-1} 1^2}{2!} = \frac{e^{-1}}{2} \approx 0.1839</me>
                
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise label="ex-poisson-panel-flaws" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Manufacturing Defects</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Microscopic flaws in manufactured carbon-fiber panels occur randomly, and independently, at an average rate of 5 flaws per 270 square feet. A technician inspects a rectangular panel measuring 9 feet by 13 feet. Let <m>Z</m> denote the total number of structural flaws contained within this specific panel.  
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Finding Lambda -->
    <task xml:id="task-panel-find-lambda">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the expected number of flaws for this specific 9 by 13-foot panel, as well as the standard deviation. Write your answers as decimals rounded to three decimal places.
             </p>
             <p>
        <m>\mu_Z=</m> <fillin width="8" answer="2.167" name="m"/> flaws.
             </p>
             <p>
        <m>\sigma_Z=</m> <fillin width="8" answer="1.472" name="sd"/> flaws.
      </p>

      
    </statement>
  
 <evaluation>
            <evaluate submit="m"><test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
             <feedback>
            <p> Correct!
             First, calculate the total surface area of the panel by multiplying its length and width:
                <me>Area = 9 \text{ ft} \times 13 \text{ ft} = 117 \text{ sq ft}</me>
                Next, scale the 270 square feet flaw rate down to a 117 square feet rate: 
                <me>\lambda =  \frac{5 \text{ flaws}}{270 \text{ sq ft}} \cdot 117 \text{ sq ft} \approx 2.167</me>
            </p>
            <p>
             This is the answer since <m>\mu_Z=E(Z)=\lambda</m> for a Poisson random variable.
            </p>
          </feedback>
            </test></evaluate>
             <evaluate submit="sd"><test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
             <feedback>
            <p>
             Correct! For a Poisson random variable <m>E(Z)=V(Z)=\lambda</m>. So, the standard deviation will be <m>\sigma_Z=\sqrt{\lambda}</m>.
            </p>
            
          </feedback>
            </test></evaluate>
        </evaluation>

    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Compute Probability -->
    <task xml:id="task-panel-compute-prob">
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Given that the panel contains at least 1 flaw, compute the conditional probability that the panel  contains at most 3 flaws. Use your  rate parameter from part (a). Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three decimal places.
                     </p>  <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.803"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p> Correct!
         Use the conditional probability formula and then the complement rule (denominator): <me>P(Z\le 3 | Z\ge 1)=\frac{P(1\le Z \le 3)}{P(Z\ge 1)} = \frac{P(Z =1)+P(Z=2)+P(Z=3) }{1-P(Z=0)}</me>
         Then use the Poisson probability distribution function with  <m>\lambda=2.167</m> to compute the required individual probabilities.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
       </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
      
    </task>
</exercise>
   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="hw-poisson-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>

  


    <exercise label="ex-static-bitcoin-poisson">
        <title>Cryptographic Hashing</title>
<introduction>
    <p>
        Bitcoin mining utilizes an algorithmic process called cryptographic hashing, which structurally behaves like a sequence of millions of independent Bernoulli trials. Each time a computing node performs a single hash, it has a tiny probability of <m>p = 1.15 \times 10^{-23}</m> of successfully solving the current network difficulty puzzle. Solving a block rewards the miner with a subsidy of <m>3.125</m> Bitcoins. 
    </p>
    <p>
        These operations are executed by specialized Application-Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC) computers called miners. A typical enterprise miner performs approximately <m>9.3 \times 10^{13}</m> hashes per second. Suppose a commercial operation deploys a farm array of <m>10</m> identical miners and runs them continuously for exactly one year.
    </p>
    </introduction>
        <!-- Part A: Total Trials Calculation -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-total-hashes">
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Determine the total cumulative number of hashes performed by the 10-miner farm array over the course of a full 365-day year.
                </p>
            </statement>
          <!--  <solution>
                <p>
                    To find the total number of independent trials (<m>n</m>), we scale the hashrate per second by the number of seconds in a standard year across the full equipment cluster:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>n \amp = (9.3 \times 10^{13} \text{ hashes/sec}) \cdot 60 \text{ sec/min} \cdot 60 \text{ min/hr} \cdot 24 \text{ hr/day} \cdot 365 \text{ days/yr} \cdot 10 \text{ miners}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 9.3 \times 10^{13} \cdot 31,536,000 \cdot 10</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 2.933 \times 10^{22} \text{ total hashes}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The total number of trials executed by the hardware cluster in one year is <m>2.933 \times 10^{22}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution> -->
        </task>

        <!-- Part B: Binomial Distribution Setup -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-binomial-parameters">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>X</m> represent the exact number of blocks successfully mined by the farm array during the year. Explain why <m>X</m> can be modeled as a Binomial random variable, and define its distribution parameters.
                </p>
            </statement>
           <!-- <solution>
                <p>
                    The random variable <m>X</m> satisfies all core conditions of a Binomial distribution: there is a fixed number of trials (<m>n</m>), each individual hash has a binary outcome (either the block solution is valid or invalid), the trials are physically independent based on the SHA-256 network protocol design, and the target success probability (<m>p</m>) remains constant across every roll.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The appropriate modeling parameters derived from our baseline system are:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Total Trials: <m>n = 2.933 \times 10^{22}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Success Probability: <m>p = 1.15 \times 10^{-23}</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution> -->
        </task>

        <!-- Part C: Poisson Approximation Parameter -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-poisson-lambda">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the Binomial model defined in part (b) can be well approximated by a Poisson distribution, and calculate the appropriate value for the rate parameter <m>\lambda</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
          <!--  <solution>
                <p>
                    Evaluating exact Binomial formulas using an enormous trial footprint (<m>n \approx 2.9 \times 10^{22}</m>) paired with an extremely small success weight (<m>p \approx 1.1 \times 10^{-23}</m>) causes direct computer arithmetic overflow failures. Because <m>n</m> is infinitely large and <m>p</m> is vanishingly close to zero, the distribution shape converges perfectly to a stable **Poisson approximation** using the expected rate scale parameter <m>\lambda = n \cdot p</m>:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\lambda \amp = n \cdot p</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = (2.933 \times 10^{22}) \cdot (1.15 \times 10^{-23})</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp = 0.3373 \text{ expected blocks per year}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The corresponding Poisson rate parameter is exactly <m>\lambda = 0.3373</m>.
                </p>
            </solution> -->
        </task>

        <!-- Part D: Complement Probability Analysis -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-prob-at-least-one">
            
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Using your Poisson approximation model, determine the  probability that the hardware cluster successfully mines exactly one block over the course of the year.
                </p>
            </statement>
          <!--  <solution>
                <p>0.24 is correct.

                    To compute <m>P(X \ge 1)</m> efficiently, we apply the standard **Complement Rule**: <m>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)</m>. First, isolate the baseline probability of mining absolutely zero blocks using our Poisson PMF:
                    <me>P(X = 0) = \frac{\lambda^0 \cdot e^{-\lambda}}{0!} = \frac{1 \cdot e^{-0.3373}}{1} \approx 0.7137</me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Subtracting this empty outcome space from total certainty:
                    <me>P(X \ge 1) = 1 - P(X = 0) = 1 - 0.7137 = 0.2863</me>
                    The probability that the farm array secures at least one block reward over the year is approximately <strong>0.2863</strong> (or 28.63%).
                </p>
            </solution> -->
        </task>

        <!-- Part E: Bounded Interval Evaluation -->
        <task xml:id="task-bitcoin-prob-interval">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the probability that the hardware cluster secures between 2 and 4 blocks, inclusive, over the annual tracking window.
                </p>
            </statement>
